<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Damghan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Damghan is a city in Semnan province, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the old road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. It holds the Tarikhaneh, one of the oldest surviving mosques in Iran. In 1729 Nader, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, defeated the Afghan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak">Ashraf Hotak</a> near the city, beginning the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damghan.jpg" alt="Damghan" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: حسن اثباتی لواسانی · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damghan-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Damghan lies 342 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> on the high road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, at an elevation of 1,250 m, and serves as capital of Damghan County and its Central District. The historical town was called Qumis, after a province of the same name stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabzevar">Sabzevar</a> to Garmsar, and up to the first century CE Damghan was the capital of that province. The name is derived from deh, village, and moghan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>, a name given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>. Tradition ascribes the founding of the town to Hooshang, the great-grandson of Keyumars and founder of the Pishdadi dynasty. Its climate is cold semi-arid. The city is known for pistachios and for paper almonds with very thin shells.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavation at Tepe Hissar, in the village of Heydarabad southeast of the city, shows that the area was inhabited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a> period, in the fifth millennium BCE, with metal production in its earliest layers. Its layers belong to several periods, including the Median, Achaemenid, Seleucid, Parthian and Sasanian, and it reached its peak in the Seleucid and Parthian periods. Ernst Herzfeld and Erich Schmidt were the first archaeologists to explore it. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, the nearby city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatompylos">Hecatompylos</a>, meaning hundred gates, was the main population centre.</p><p>Damghan was half destroyed in the earthquake of 856 and was an important city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, though few remains of that time survive. It was capital of the province of Qumis until it was destroyed by the Afghan Hotak dynasty in 1723. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath-Ali Shah Qajar</a> was born in a citadel in the western part of the city in 1772, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> rulers built palaces at the spring of Cheshmeh-Ali, 30 km to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Damghan is known for its monuments. The Tarikhaneh Mosque was built over a Sasanian fire temple and is one of the oldest mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with massive columns, wood carvings and two minarets of the 11th century. Many Seljuk buildings survive, including the Gunbad-i Chihil Dukhtaran mausoleum, built in AH 466 (1087 CE) and 14.8 m high, the shrine of Imamzade Ja'far and the tomb of Pir-i Alamdar. North of the city stand the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> fortresses of Gerdkuh, said to have been used by Hassan Sabah and conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a>, and Mehrnegar on Mansourkuh. Sections of the old walls, some from the Sasanian era, remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Damghan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated the Ghilzai Afghans at <b>Damghan</b> and Murche-Khort and expelled them from Isfahan in 1729, freeing the Safavid capital after seven years of occupation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of estimated magnitude 7.9 struck near <b>Damghan</b>, capital of the province of Qumis in the Abbasid Caliphate, with its worst shaking along some 350 kilometres of the eastern Alborz.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nader Shah, fighting for the Safavid pretender Tahmasp II, defeated Ashraf at <b>Damghan</b> in 1729 and drove the Afghans out of Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/hotak-dynasty">Hotak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
